@page "/blogs/blazor-wasm-forms-validation"
@using BaseComponents
@using BlazorDemoComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(Validation)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                Can your Blazor form catch an invalid booking item as well as an empty customer name?
                <Highlight>Data annotations</Highlight> let you declare validation rules on a model, and
                <Highlight>DataAnnotationsValidator</Highlight> connects those rules to a Blazor WebAssembly form.
            </p>
            <p>
                Start with a small client form, then build an Anybills-inspired booking form with custom rules and
                collection items, nested contact details, and lookup-only data that should skip validation.
                The complex example uses validation metadata generated at build time in .NET 10 and later.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                A booking can have a valid customer but an invalid item quantity, contact email, or end time before its start.
                If you only check the top-level fields, a form submission can miss invalid nested objects and collection items.
                Without generated metadata, <Highlight>DataAnnotationsValidator</Highlight> falls back to top-level
                model validation on submit; missing metadata doesn't produce a runtime error.
            </p>
            <p>
                Field messages help users correct input where they entered it, while a summary shows errors across the form.
                A partial catalogue record has a different purpose: treating it as a booking item could block an otherwise valid form.
                Client-side checks improve feedback, but your server must independently validate submitted data before processing it.
                The examples below show how to validate user input while excluding lookup-only data, without a separate FluentValidation integration.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4 id="validate-a-client-name">Validate a client name with built-in attributes</h4>
            <p>
                Add <Highlight>Required</Highlight> and <Highlight>MaxLength</Highlight> to the name, then put
                <Highlight>DataAnnotationsValidator</Highlight> inside the <Highlight>EditForm</Highlight>.
                This self-contained example follows the simple demo; it needs no generated metadata for its top-level field.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
@@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations

&lt;EditForm Model="@@exampleModel" OnValidSubmit="@@HandleValidSubmit"&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;
    &lt;ValidationSummary /&gt;

    &lt;label for="name"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;InputText id="name" @@bind-Value="exampleModel.Name" /&gt;
    &lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; exampleModel.Name)" /&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;@@message&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/EditForm&gt;

@@code {
    public class ExampleModel
    {
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Name is required.")]
        [MaxLength(50, ErrorMessage = "Name too long (50 character limit).")]
        public string? Name { get; set; }
    }

    private ExampleModel exampleModel = new();
    private string? message;

    private void HandleValidSubmit()
    {
        message = "HandleValidSubmit called";
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>ValidationMessage</Highlight> displays errors for the named field; <Highlight>ValidationSummary</Highlight>
                displays form-wide errors. Field validation runs when the field changes, and
                <Highlight>OnValidSubmit</Highlight> runs only after full-form validation succeeds.
                The success text in this demo remains visible after a later invalid submission; it isn't a live validity indicator.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
Name input       | Submit result
-----------------+---------------------------------
empty            | Name is required.
51 characters    | Name too long (50 character limit).
Taylor           | HandleValidSubmit called
            </CodeSnippet>
            <DemoSnippet Title="Simple data annotations validation">
                <p class="[ text-black ] [ dark:text-white ]">
                    Submit an empty name, enter more than 50 characters, then try a valid name.
                </p>
                <div>
                    <ValidationDemo></ValidationDemo>
                </div>
            </DemoSnippet>

            <h4 id="enable-generated-validation">Enable generated validation for nested objects and booking items</h4>
            <p>
                Register <Highlight>AddValidation</Highlight> in the assembly that declares the booking model.
                The call registers validation services and activates a source generator, which produces metadata at build time.
                Put the root and child model types in a <Highlight>.cs</Highlight> file, not a Razor component, and mark the root
                with <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight>; the generator discovers reachable child types.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
builder.Services.AddValidation();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The one-line registration works when the model and the call live in the same project.
                If models live in a separate assembly, put an extension there to run the source generator,
                then call that extension from the host before building the service provider. In this demo,
                the model and registration extension live in the eagerly loaded <Highlight>SharedModels</Highlight>
                assembly. The UI remains in the lazy-loaded <Highlight>BlazorDemoComponents</Highlight> assembly;
                calling its extension during host startup would fail because that assembly hasn't been loaded yet.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

namespace SharedModels;

public static class ValidationServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddSharedModelValidation(
        this IServiceCollection services)
    {
        services.AddValidation();
        return services;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The WebAssembly host calls the extension before building its service provider:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
builder.Services.AddSharedModelValidation();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Add a reference to <Highlight>Microsoft.Extensions.Validation</Highlight> if it isn't available in the
                model project; use a version appropriate for your target framework. In .NET 10,
                <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight> and <Highlight>SkipValidation</Highlight> are experimental package APIs.
                Web and Razor SDK projects containing Razor components provide embedded alternatives; plain class libraries
                need the documented workaround or diagnostic suppression. See Microsoft's
                <NavLink href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/validation?view=aspnetcore-10.0#experimental-api-in-apps-that-target-net-10" target="_blank">.NET 10 guidance</NavLink>.
            </p>

            <h4 id="express-booking-rules">Express booking and item rules in the model</h4>
            <p>
                Put the model in <Highlight>ValidationModels.cs</Highlight> in the same assembly as the registration call.
                These focused excerpts show the root model, its collection, and the item rules; the live demo contains the complete
                model. Initialize a <Highlight>DateTimeOffset</Highlight> from <Highlight>DateTime.Today</Highlight>, not
                <Highlight>DateTimeOffset.Today</Highlight>, which doesn't exist. The end-date method below demonstrates
                cross-property validation.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
[ValidatableType]
public sealed class BookingValidationModel
{
    [CustomValidation(typeof(BookingValidationModel), nameof(ValidateCustomer))]
    public Guid CustomerId { get; set; }

    [CustomValidation(typeof(BookingValidationModel), nameof(ValidateStart))]
    public DateTimeOffset StartAt { get; set; } = new(DateTime.Today.AddDays(1).AddHours(9));

    [CustomValidation(typeof(BookingValidationModel), nameof(ValidateEnd))]
    public DateTimeOffset EndAt { get; set; } = new(DateTime.Today.AddDays(1).AddHours(10));

    [CustomValidation(
        typeof(BookingValidationModel), nameof(ValidateBookingItems))]
    public List&lt;BookingItemValidationModel&gt; BookingItems { get; set; } =
        [new()];
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The full model also validates the room, notes, and each collection item. Its
                <Highlight>BookingItemValidationModel</Highlight> applies these rules to every item:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public sealed class BookingItemValidationModel
{
    [Required]
    public string? Name { get; set; }

    [Range(1, 100, ErrorMessage = "Quantity must be between 1 and 100.")]
    public int Quantity { get; set; }

    [CustomValidation(
        typeof(BookingItemValidationModel), nameof(ValidateStaff))]
    public Guid StaffId { get; set; }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>CustomValidation</Highlight> calls a static method when a built-in attribute doesn't express a rule.
                The customer, room, and staff checks reject <Highlight>Guid.Empty</Highlight>; a nonempty identifier alone does
                not prove that the selected record exists or is authorized.
                The list rule requires at least one item, while generated metadata enables validation of each item's properties.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public static ValidationResult? ValidateCustomer(Guid value) =&gt;
    value == Guid.Empty
        ? new("Customer is required.", [nameof(CustomerId)])
        : ValidationResult.Success;

public static ValidationResult? ValidateBookingItems(
    List&lt;BookingItemValidationModel&gt; value) =&gt;
    value.Count == 0
        ? new("At least one booking item is required.",
            [nameof(BookingItems)])
        : ValidationResult.Success;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>ValidationContext.ObjectInstance</Highlight> gives the end-date rule access to the booking's start.
                Returning member names with <Highlight>ValidationResult</Highlight> associates errors with their fields.
                For a cross-field rule, changing the start alone doesn't guarantee that the end field is immediately revalidated:
                submit the form to recheck the complete model, or explicitly revalidate dependent fields in a production form.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public static ValidationResult? ValidateEnd(
    DateTimeOffset value, ValidationContext context)
{
    if (value == DateTimeOffset.MinValue)
        return new("End date is required.", [nameof(EndAt)]);

    var booking = (BookingValidationModel)context.ObjectInstance;
    return booking.StartAt != DateTimeOffset.MinValue &amp;&amp; value &lt;= booking.StartAt
        ? new("End must be after Start.", [nameof(EndAt)])
        : ValidationResult.Success;
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4 id="validate-nested-contact">Validate contact fields through the root model</h4>
            <p>
                Add an initialized <Highlight>Contact</Highlight> property to <Highlight>BookingValidationModel</Highlight>.
                These excerpts belong in <Highlight>SharedModels/ValidationModels.cs</Highlight>, alongside the existing rules;
                keep <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight> on the root.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Inside BookingValidationModel:
[Required]
public BookingContactValidationModel Contact { get; set; } = new();

// Separate child class in the same file:
public sealed class BookingContactValidationModel
{
    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Contact email is required.")]
    [EmailAddress(ErrorMessage = "Enter a valid contact email.")]
    public string? Email { get; set; }

    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Contact phone is required.")]
    [StringLength(20, MinimumLength = 6,
        ErrorMessage = "Contact phone must be 6 - 20 characters.")]
    public string? Phone { get; set; }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The child needs no <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight> attribute because the generator reaches it through
                the annotated root. <Highlight>Required</Highlight> on <Highlight>Contact</Highlight> rejects a null reference;
                it doesn't recursively validate the child's properties on its own.
                Generated metadata enables that traversal on whole-form submission.
            </p>
            <p>
                Bind each contact message to the nested property, not the root's <Highlight>Contact</Highlight> reference.
                Insert this markup inside the booking <Highlight>EditForm</Highlight>, after its validator and summary:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
&lt;label for="contact-email"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;InputText id="contact-email" @@bind-Value="booking.Contact.Email" /&gt;
&lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; booking.Contact.Email)" /&gt;

&lt;label for="contact-phone"&gt;Phone&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;InputText id="contact-phone" @@bind-Value="booking.Contact.Phone" /&gt;
&lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; booking.Contact.Phone)" /&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Submit with blank contact fields to see both required messages beside the inputs and in the summary.
                Then submit with <Highlight>not-an-email</Highlight> and phone <Highlight>123</Highlight> to see the format and
                length errors. Use <Highlight>taylor@example.com</Highlight> and <Highlight>123456</Highlight> to satisfy these rules;
                the phone rule checks length, not whether the number exists.
            </p>

            <h4 id="skip-lookup-validation">Skip lookup data without skipping actual booking items</h4>
            <p>
                Apply <Highlight>SkipValidation</Highlight> to <Highlight>AvailableItems</Highlight> on the root model.
                The attribute comes from <Highlight>Microsoft.Extensions.Validation</Highlight>, alongside
                <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight>; the rule attributes come from
                <Highlight>System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Inside BookingValidationModel:
[SkipValidation]
public List&lt;BookingItemValidationModel&gt; AvailableItems { get; set; } = [];
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Anybills stores lookup items in a dictionary. This smaller demo uses a list and deliberately reuses
                <Highlight>BookingItemValidationModel</Highlight> so both collections have the same traversable item type:
                one is skipped, and the other is validated. A production catalogue can use a separate item view model.
                Its read-only catalogue preview contains this intentionally incomplete entry:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using SharedModels;

var booking = new BookingValidationModel
{
    AvailableItems = [new() { Name = "Consultation" }]
};
// Lookup value defaults: Quantity = 0, StaffId = Guid.Empty.
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                This list is lookup-only: users don't edit it, and the demo doesn't submit it to a server.
                Generated validation skips the list and its items, so the incomplete catalogue entry doesn't block
                <Highlight>OnValidSubmit</Highlight>. Keep <Highlight>BookingItems</Highlight> validated: actual items still
                require a name, quantity from 1 to 100, and a staff selection.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>SkipValidation</Highlight> is not a trust, sanitization, or security boundary.
                It doesn't remove data from serialization or exempt submitted data from server validation.
                In a real application, send only the intended booking data and independently validate it on the server.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
BookingValidationModel
  |-- CustomerId, RoomId, StartAt, EndAt, Notes
  |-- Contact             [Required] rejects null
  |    `-- Email, Phone   child rules via generated traversal
  |-- AvailableItems      [SkipValidation]: no value traversal
  `-- BookingItems         list rule
       `-- each item       Name, Quantity, StaffId rules
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4 id="display-booking-errors">Show collection and field errors in the booking form</h4>
            <p>
                Bind each message to the same model instance and property as its input. This focused excerpt shows a collection
                item; the complete runnable component, including the customer and date fields, is
                <Highlight>BookingValidationDemo.razor</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
@@using SharedModels

&lt;EditForm Model="@@booking" OnValidSubmit="@@HandleValidSubmit"&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;
    &lt;ValidationSummary /&gt;

    &lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; booking.BookingItems)" /&gt;
    @@foreach (var item in booking.BookingItems)
    {
        &lt;div @@key="item"&gt;
            &lt;InputText @@bind-Value="item.Name" /&gt;
            &lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; item.Name)" /&gt;
            &lt;InputNumber @@bind-Value="item.Quantity" /&gt;
            &lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; item.Quantity)" /&gt;
            &lt;InputSelect @@bind-Value="item.StaffId"&gt;
                &lt;option value="@@Guid.Empty"&gt;Select staff&lt;/option&gt;
                &lt;option value="@@staffId"&gt;Alex Kim&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;/InputSelect&gt;
            &lt;ValidationMessage For="@@(() =&gt; item.StaffId)" /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    }
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save booking&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/EditForm&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Submit after adding or removing items to recheck the list and its contents. As in the simple demo,
                the success text records a previous successful submission rather than the current validity state.
                The local identifiers and the callback are illustrative: they don't load records or save a booking.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
Input                           | Expected result on submit
--------------------------------+---------------------------------
No customer                     | Customer is required.
No booking items                | At least one booking item is required.
Item quantity 0                 | Quantity must be between 1 and 100.
End at or before start          | End must be after Start.
Contact email empty             | Contact email is required.
Contact email not-an-email      | Enter a valid contact email.
Contact phone empty             | Contact phone is required.
Contact phone 123               | Contact phone must be 6 - 20 characters.
Lookup quantity 0, no staff     | No lookup errors (SkipValidation).
All editable fields valid       | Booking passed validation.
            </CodeSnippet>
            <DemoSnippet Title="Complex booking validation">
                <p class="[ text-black ] [ dark:text-white ]">
                    Use Save booking after each change below to observe whole-form validation.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Leave Email and Phone blank and submit. Check both the nested field messages and the summary.</li>
                    <li>
                        Enter <Highlight>not-an-email</Highlight> and phone <Highlight>123</Highlight>, then submit.
                        Replace them with <Highlight>taylor@example.com</Highlight> and <Highlight>123456</Highlight>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Select a customer and room, keep End after Start, and give the booking item a name,
                        quantity <Highlight>1</Highlight>, and a staff member. Submit: expect
                        <Highlight>Booking passed validation.</Highlight> even though the catalogue preview still shows
                        quantity <Highlight>0</Highlight> and no staff.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Change the editable booking item's quantity to <Highlight>0</Highlight> or clear its staff selection
                        and submit. Errors now appear for that item, not the lookup entry.
                        Remove all items and submit to see the list-level rule; add and complete an item to restore validity.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <div>
                    <BookingValidationDemo></BookingValidationDemo>
                </div>
            </DemoSnippet>
            <p>
                These examples adapt patterns from the Anybills <Highlight>net11</Highlight> branch:
                the branch form's nested contact, and the booking form's separation of lookup data from editable items.
                For framework details, see Microsoft's
                <NavLink href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/forms/validation?view=aspnetcore-10.0" target="_blank">Blazor validation documentation</NavLink>.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <ul>
                <li>Use <Highlight>DataAnnotationsValidator</Highlight> and field messages for a simple form.</li>
                <li>Generate metadata from the root to validate nested contact fields and collection items; reachable children need no extra <Highlight>ValidatableType</Highlight>.</li>
                <li>Use <Highlight>CustomValidation</Highlight> for cross-property and collection rules.</li>
                <li>Use <Highlight>SkipValidation</Highlight> only for lookup-only data, never to bypass checks on actual booking items.</li>
                <li>Recheck dependent fields and enforce business rules independently on the server.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                What to read next: revisit <NavLink href="blogs/blazor-wasm-forms">Blazor WebAssembly forms</NavLink>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
